  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated] | Tom's Hardware\nNvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\nBy Luke James\nLeaving Nvidia without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra past its current",
  "passage": "a, with trace spacing at or below 25μm and impedance held within a tolerance of 5% to keep 448 Gb/s-class signaling intact. A cabled version of the same interconnect would need upward of 20,000 discrete cables, which is why Nvidia is moving the wiring onto a single passive board.\nNVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit. NVL576, a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip too or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures.\nThese cancellations leave Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" meaning the largest single Rubin Ultra domain in 2027 could match, but not exceed, what Oberon already delivers.\nNvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part last week over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute. SemiAnalysis has also placed a fully production-ready co-packaged optics NVSwitch no earlier than the Feynman generation that follows Rubin, which leaves copper as the only near-term solution for linking Rubin Ultra at rack scale and thereby puts even more weight on the PCB midplane.\nThe delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay. We've reached out to Nvidia for comment.\n2028? Maybe some data centres will",
  "statement": "These cancellations leave Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" meaning the largest single Rubin Ultra domain in 2027 could match, but not exceed, what Oberon already delivers."
 },
 {
  "id": "U30",
  "title": "Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated] | Tom's Hardware\nNvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\nBy Luke James\nLeaving Nvidia without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra past its current",
  "passage": "s in a current Oberon NVL72 rack . Every GPU-to-GPU link inside the cabinet runs through that board, and copper traces lose signal integrity as layer counts increase, alongside power delivery and thermal design challenges. Jensen Huang held up the gray backplane on stage at GTC back in March.\nTrade analyses of the board describe three 26-layer sections laminated into one 78-layer stack close to a square meter in area, with trace spacing at or below 25μm and impedance held within a tolerance of 5% to keep 448 Gb/s-class signaling intact. A cabled version of the same interconnect would need upward of 20,000 discrete cables, which is why Nvidia is moving the wiring onto a single passive board.\nNVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit. NVL576, a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip too or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures.\nThese cancellations leave Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" meaning the largest single Rubin Ultra domain in 2027 could match, but not exceed, what Oberon already delivers.\nNvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part last week over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute. SemiAnalysis has also placed a fully production-ready co-packaged optics NVSwitch no earlier than the Feynman generation that follows Rub",
  "statement": "NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit."
 },
 {
  "id": "U31",
  "title": "LG Electronics to Build Nvidia AI Server Racks",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-12",
  "article_opening": "By Lee Seok-jin\nLG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. The move expands the recent AI infrastructure alliance between LG and Nvidia into server rack manufacturing.\nAccording to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \"Vera Rubin\" in the first half ",
  "passage": "By Lee Seok-jin\nLG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. The move expands the recent AI infrastructure alliance between LG and Nvidia into server rack manufacturing.\nAccording to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \"Vera Rubin\" in the first half of the year. LG plans to secure orders from global big tech companies after reliability evaluations in the second half and begin mass production next year. The research and development was conducted at a 1.8-megawatt data center cluster established at LG PRI's Pyeongtaek site.\nA compute tray is a module that integrates computing units such as graphics processing units (GPUs) into a single board, with dozens of trays stacked vertically to form a single rack. In the Vera Rubin rack, a total of 108 AI chips operate simultaneously, generating enormous heat, so how quickly and stably the heat is cooled determines the performance of the AI server rack.\nLG Electronics has transferred the cooling technology acc",
  "statement": "According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \"Vera Rubin\" in the first half of the year."
 },
 {
  "id": "U32",
  "title": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips use",
  "passage": "2025 after launching a low-cost, high-performance AI model that disrupted the global technology industry.\nThe Hangzhou-based company has so far been better known for focusing on AI model innovation rather than commercialising the technology it develops.\nAmid US restrictions on AI chip exports to China, Huawei has expanded its domestic market share and now controls around half of China's AI chip market, which is valued at approximately US$50 billion.\nHowever, that dominance is increasingly being challenged as several local technology companies, including Alibaba and Baidu, develop their own AI chips.\nAccording to Reuters sources, DeepSeek's chip development project remains at an early stage. Over the past year, the company has been communicating with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers to support the development of the semiconductors.\nDeepSeek has also increased recruitment of chip design engineers in recent months. The hiring process has been conducted discreetly, without publicly posting job openings on general recruitment platforms.\nDespite becoming one of the symbols of China's AI resurgence, DeepSeek has maintained a low profile and has not responded to Reuters' request for confirmation regarding the project.\nGlobally, DeepSeek's move follows a broader trend among major AI companies developing their own chips. OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, last month in collaboration with Broadcom, while Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.\nFor DeepSeek, chip developmen",
  "statement": "Over the past year, the company has been communicating with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers to support the development of the semiconductors."
 },
 {
  "id": "U33",
  "title": "Nvidia vs. Strategy: Which Stock Has a Better Chance of Surging 10x by 2036?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) needs no introduction. It's at the center of the artificial intelligence (AI) boom, supplying industry-leading hardware and software for the infrastructure build-out.\nInvestors might have heard of Strategy ( MSTR -3.06% ) . It's a $32 billion enterprise software business that morphed into a Bitcoin treasury company.\nBetween these two stocks, whose recent performances are on opposite ends of the spectrum, which has a better chance of surging 10x by 2036?\nNvidia's incredible",
  "passage": "or the infrastructure build-out.\nInvestors might have heard of Strategy ( MSTR -3.06% ) . It's a $32 billion enterprise software business that morphed into a Bitcoin treasury company.\nBetween these two stocks, whose recent performances are on opposite ends of the spectrum, which has a better chance of surging 10x by 2036?\nNvidia's incredible financial gains have launched the stock\nNvidia has been the most impressive story in the economy and stock market. Its shares are up an astonishing 897% in the past five years (as of June 30). When investors think of the AI revolution, there's no doubt this business is what comes to mind first. The company's financial success is impossible to overstate.\nJust in the last three years, from Q1 2024 to the first quarter of fiscal 2027 (ended April 26), Nvidia's revenue soared 1,033%. Its graphics processing units, used to operate AI data centers, are perhaps the hottest piece of hardware the world has ever seen. Demand has been exceptional as Nvidia's hyperscaler customers race to build computing capacity to meet their clients' needs.\nPhenomenal top-line growth has boosted the company's profit. Net income totaled $58.3 billion in the most recent fiscal quarter. That metric was just $2 billion exactly three years before, translating to a monster 29-fold three-year rise. Nvidia's pricing power plays a role here, as its gross margin was 74.9% during Q1.\nNvidia has a wide economic moat. The company clearly excels in hardware, but its CUDA software platform raises switching ",
  "statement": "Just in the last three years, from Q1 2024 to the first quarter of fiscal 2027 (ended April 26), Nvidia's revenue soared 1,033%."
 },
 {
  "id": "U34",
  "title": "Chinese firms leave Nvidia for local AI suppliers, survey shows",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "[SINGAPORE] Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators in favour of domestic silicon, underscoring how tensions with the US are reshaping the artificial intelligence infrastructure buildout and propelling Beijing’s ambitions to substitute American technology.\nExecutives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\nThis is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence surve",
  "passage": " as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\nChina is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\nAt least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\nBut a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to reap the benefits.\nThe bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report. BLOOMBERG",
  "statement": "The bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report."
 },
 {
  "id": "U35",
  "title": "DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-09",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek has already rattled Nvidia Corporation (NVDA) investors once with its low-cost artificial intelligence (AI) models. Now, the Chinese startup may be posing a far more direct competitive threat. Reports that DeepSeek is developing its own inference-focused AI chip could reduce its reliance on Nvidia's GPUs, highlighting a broader industry trend in which leading AI developers are increasingly designing custom silicon to lower costs, improve efficiency, and gain greater control over their A",
  "passage": "mous driving, robotics, and AI-enabled edge devices.\nManagement also pointed to accelerating adoption of Blackwell systems and highlighted expanding opportunities in agentic AI and enterprise AI infrastructure.\nAdditionally, Nvidia issued another optimistic outlook for the second quarter of fiscal 2027, forecasting revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%. The guidance assumes no contribution from China Data Center compute revenue due to ongoing U.S. export restrictions.\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 90.2% YOY to $8.69 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 34.3% increase to $11.67 in fiscal 2028.\nLast month, Morgan Stanley reaffirmed its \"Overweight\" rating on the stock and maintained its $288 price target, reiterating Nvidia as its top pick in the processor space.\nPlus, Truist Securities reiterated its \"Buy\" rating and $307 price target after Nvidia unveiled several next-generation AI products at GTC Taipei.\nOverall, NVDA has a consensus \"Strong Buy\" rating. Of the 49 analysts covering the stock, 43 advise a \"Strong Buy,\" three suggest a \"Moderate Buy,\" two analysts give it a \"Hold\" rating, and one offers a \"Strong Sell\" rating.\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $301.92, indicating a potential upside of 47.9%. Also, the Street-high target price of $500 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 145%.",
  "statement": "Last month, Morgan Stanley reaffirmed its \"Overweight\" rating on the stock and maintained its $288 price target, reiterating Nvidia as its top pick in the processor space."
 },
 {
  "id": "U36",
  "title": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\nA report from analyst firm SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack has been delayed by more than a year. Suppliers across Japan, Taiwan, South Korea, and Hong Kong saw their stock prices drop sharply in response.\nAsian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because",
  "passage": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\nA report from analyst firm SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack has been delayed by more than a year. Suppliers across Japan, Taiwan, South Korea, and Hong Kong saw their stock prices drop sharply in response.\nAsian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\nThe issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 just three months earlier at the company's GTC conference.\nThe report hit an already jittery investor base. After years of AI-fueled gains, even small setbacks trigger sharp sell-offs. Japanese PCB maker Ibiden, which counts Nvidia as its largest customer, dropped as much as ten percent. Kingboard Laminates fell 18 percent in Hong Kong, Elite Material lost ten percent in Taiwan, and Samsung Electro-Mechanics slid eleven percent in South Korea. The sell-off follows massive run-ups. Samsung Electro-Mechanics had gained more than 600 percent this year, and Kingboard Laminates more than 470 percent.\nScrapped designs give competitors an opening\n",
  "statement": "The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects."
 },
 {
  "id": "U37",
  "title": "DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-09",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek has already rattled Nvidia Corporation (NVDA) investors once with its low-cost artificial intelligence (AI) models. Now, the Chinese startup may be posing a far more direct competitive threat. Reports that DeepSeek is developing its own inference-focused AI chip could reduce its reliance on Nvidia's GPUs, highlighting a broader industry trend in which leading AI developers are increasingly designing custom silicon to lower costs, improve efficiency, and gain greater control over their A",
  "passage": "rformance, the stock is up just 9.45% year-to-date (YTD) and 27.57% over the past 52 weeks, lagging the broader technology sector.\nThe stock is currently trading 13.7% below its 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. Investor sentiment has been pressured by concerns over the sustainability of AI infrastructure spending, intensifying competition, U.S. export restrictions, and uncertainty surrounding the rollout of next-generation AI platforms, including Rubin and Kyber.\nRecently, Nvidia faced reports that suggested its next-generation Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028. However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\nNvidia trades at 22.49 times forward earnings, which is currently a discount compared to industry peers and the historical average.\nNvidia delivered another exceptional quarter when it reported first-quarter fiscal 2027 results on May 20, further cementing its leadership in the rapidly growing AI infrastructure market.\nFor the quarter ended April 26, Nvidia posted record revenue of $81.6 billion, an 85% year-over-year (YOY) increase, while net income jumped 211% YOY to $58.32 billion. On a non-GAAP basis, earnings per share (EPS) rose 140% from the prior-year period to $1.87, beating analyst estimates. Non-GAAP gross margin expanded to 75% from 60.8% a year earlier, underscoring the company",
  "statement": "Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy."
 },
 {
  "id": "U38",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nAmazon",
  "passage": "atch hyperscaler capex mix, whether networking growth stays vertical, and any China SKU announcement over the next two quarters. Any one breaking hard could tip the call.\nNVIDIA trades at $196 against an analyst consensus target of ~$301.62 as of this writing, implying 53% upside. Coverage skews heavily positive with 10 Strong Buy, 48 Buy, 2 Hold, 1 Sell ratings. Forward P/E sits at 22x, trailing P/E at 29x.\nPerformance is mixed. NVDA is up 4.59% year to date and 24.06% over the trailing year, but down 12.46% over the past month. The S&P 500 delivered a smaller trailing-year gain, so NVDA outperformed with more turbulence.\nAt $196, NVIDIA remains a buy.\nThe numbers do not argue for selling. A company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise. The numbers also do not argue for aggressively adding. Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list, and management’s NVLink Fusion pivot is an implicit acknowledgment that fighting custom silicon head-on loses.\nBuy conviction requires durable evidence that networking and software capture margin even when compute goes custom, plus a China resolution. Sell conviction requires a hyperscaler capex reset or a Trainium/TPU disclosure that reframes NVIDIA as a supplier rather than the platform. Prediction markets show 80.5% conviction NVDA touches $192 in July and only 7% for a week close above $210, a range consistent wi",
  "statement": "A company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise."
 },
 {
  "id": "U39",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "a centers. But there is a hidden cost: the toxic supply chain that manufactures advanced semiconductors that fill those data centers. Over the past three years, Nvidia’s supply chain emissions have more than doubled , and the trend is likely to continue as the company’s GPU shipments are forecast to grow at 26 percent per year.\nNvidia outsources most of its manufacturing to Taiwanese semiconductor maker TSMC. As every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last, and as the AI frenzy is driving unprecedented demands for more GPU semiconductors, the island has been left to deal with ballooning energy demands and emissions.\nTake Fab 25 as an example. This TSMC manufacturing center, to be built near Taichung city, will require at least 1 GW of power – roughly the same as the entire city of Taichung – and about 100,000 tonnes of water per day, roughly a fifth of the city’s daily water use. Across Taiwan, TSMC operates more than two dozen fabs, and is planning 10 more advanced fabs like Fab 25. As a result, TSMC alone could consume 24 percent of Taiwan’s electricity by 2030, up from over 12 percent last year.\nTo meet this surge, Taipower, Taiwan’s utility, plans to build 18 gas plants totaling about 18.2 GW, roughly 40 percent of Taiwan’s current generating capacity, largely to meet the demand of the semiconductor industry, led by TSMC. This will further lock Taiwan into fossil-fuel dependency, even as the island already relies on fossil fuels for more than 80 percent of its electricity.\nNear Taiwan, South Korea – another major Nvidia manufacturing hub – faces a similar challe",
  "statement": "This TSMC manufacturing center, to be built near Taichung city, will require at least 1 GW of power – roughly the same as the entire city of Taichung – and about 100,000 tonnes of water per day, roughly a fifth of the city’s daily water use."
 },
